INT. GARAGE - DAY

Pegboard walls hung with outlines of tools that are no longer there. A workbench strewn with jars of screws. Open cardboard boxes, half-filled. Dust floats in a band of sunlight.

EUGENIA PRYOR, 50s, in jeans and a button-down, sorts bolts into a coffee can. Across from her, THADDEUS PRYOR, 50s, stands with a worn leather glove in his hands, turning it over.

EUGENIA
That one's Dad's.

THADDEUS
I know whose it is.

EUGENIA
Take it, if you want.

THADDEUS
I don't want it.

EUGENIA
Then put it in the donate box.

THADDEUS
I'm not putting it in the donate box.

EUGENIA
Then keep it.

THADDEUS
I said I don't want it.

She stops. She sets the can down. A long pause.

EUGENIA
What do you want, Thad?

THADDEUS
I want the afternoon to be over.

EUGENIA
It's eleven in the morning.

THADDEUS
Then I want it to be tomorrow.

She nods slowly. She goes back to the bolts.

THADDEUS (CONT'D)
Did he ever say anything to you? About the garage?

EUGENIA
He said it was the only room in the house where nobody asked him questions.

THADDEUS
That sounds like him.

EUGENIA
He called it the quiet room.

THADDEUS
He never called it that to me.

EUGENIA
You never asked.

THADDEUS
He never gave me a chance.

EUGENIA
Thad.

THADDEUS
I'm just saying. Eight years of weekends, and I'm still learning his hobbies from the pegboard.

EUGENIA
He fixed your bike once.

THADDEUS
He fixed it wrong. I rode it into a wall.

EUGENIA
He came to the hospital.

THADDEUS
For twenty minutes.

EUGENIA
For four hours. You were asleep.

THADDEUS
(a beat)
I didn't know that.

EUGENIA
Now you do.

He sets the glove on the workbench. He smooths the leather. He leaves his hand on it.

THADDEUS
I'll take the can of bolts.

EUGENIA
They're all different sizes.

THADDEUS
I know.

She hands him the can. He holds it against his chest, and nods, and does not look at her.
